  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1249 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nAlright, move on, nothing to see here... besides being a great movie \nquote also sums up our weather over the next 7+ days. If you've been \noutside today, you've likely noticed a decidedly drier airmass is in \nplace and that is thanks to the cold front that cleared the MPX area \nto the southeast this morning. We're seeing high pressure move in \nfrom Montana, with dewpoints in the Dakotas into northern MN down in \nthe 30s. As this high moves in, we'll see skies gradually clear out, \nwhich will set up a cool start to your Friday, with lows in the \nupper 30s to mid 40s.\n\nSoutherly winds develop late in the day on Thursday as the high \nmoves into WI/IL. These southerly winds will remain in place through \nthe day on Saturday before our next cold front moves through \nSaturday night. Eventually, these southerly winds will bring a \nchance at seeing some highs in the 70s on Saturday in western MN, \nbut the big thing with this front is it represents our only, and \nrather meager, chance at rain over the next 7 days. The upper wave \ndriving this front will be going across Canada, with an extension \ndown into MN. So the forcing is better to the north. Moisture is \nlack luster as we never really recover our moisture feed from \ntoday's frontal passage. Forecast soundings show very dry air in the \nlowest 8k feet or so of the atmosphere. So you add in weak forcing, \nweak moisture, no convective instability, and the inverted-v \nsoundings and it all adds up to a pretty minor rain event. We expect \nscattered showers to move through with the front Saturday night and \ndid see the NBM bump up PoPs into the 30s, but amounts will be under \na tenth of an inch. The good news is these rain chances will come \nlong after any Little Brown Jugs are earned.\n\nBehind this front, we get high pressure and pleasant weather for \nSunday, with excellent weather conditions for things like any runs \nor tailgating adventures you may have planned for what will be a \nbusy weekend of events in the Twin Cities.\n\nNext week, we'll see above normal heights spread across the CONUS, \nwith the upper jet retreating into Canada. This is a classic setup \nfor seeing mild and dry Pacific airmasses overspreading the CONUS \nand that's exactly what we'll see. The high pressure from Sunday \nwill be in the Great Lakes by the start of next week, with warm \nsoutherly winds overspreading the region. Add in a shortwave \nmoving across Canada mid-week helping to draw warm air north and\nwe have what should be a beautiful stretch of weather next \nweek, with highs in the 70s starting Tuesday. There are no \nindications of any stronger fronts and changes to this airmass \nuntil next weekend, though right now, deterministic, machine \nlearning, and ensemble models alike show this being another dry \nfropa.",
